# data file for fldtk fluid (Fast Light User Interface Designer)
version 1.0000
comment {The interactive editor's widget palette ("Tools" window).

Ported from FLTK's fluid/panels/function_panel.fl -- only the
`make_widgetbin()` window (`widgetbin_panel`, renamed `widgetBinPanel`
here for this project's own camelCase convention), but now the FULL
window: all 8 group boxes, all 57 buttons, every one at FLTK's own
literal `xywh` slot. Window size (600x102) is FLTK's own too.

Each button is a `BinButton` (`class \{BinButton\}`, real `tooltip`,
`setup \{o.typeName("X");\}`, `callback binButtonCb`) whose type is
registered in both `fluid.factory` (Node-parsing side) and
`fluid.instantiate` (live-widget side) -- click to add at a default
position, or drag onto the canvas to drop at the exact release
point via real XDND. See `fluid/bin_button.d`'s own doc
comment for the click-vs-drag mechanism. The Code group's own 9
buttons are wired to a second delegate, `callback codeButtonCb` --
they still reuse `BinButton` for its `typeName()` storage, but a
`Function`/`decl`/`comment`/... node is never a live canvas widget, so
only the click path (`gui_main.d`'s `addNode()`, the non-widget
counterpart of `addWidget()`) actually does anything; dragging one onto
the canvas is a harmless no-op (matches FLTK's own real split -- these 9
used a plain click-only `type_make_cb` there too, never `Bin_Button`'s
drag machinery).

The four menu-item buttons (`MenuItem`/`CheckMenuItem`/`Submenu`/
`RadioMenuItem`) are menu-structure nodes, not canvas widgets, so
`binButtonCb` routes them through `gui_main.d`'s `addNode()` (the same
path as the `&New/&Menus` entries), and a drag-and-drop of one adds
the item to the menu owner or submenu it is dropped on. The Table
button creates a `Table` showing sample data, as FLTK's does.

Each button's `setup \{\}` calls `o.image(pixmapFor("X"))` right after
`o.typeName("X")`, matching FLTK's own `o->image(pixmap_for("Fl_X"))`
one-for-one in spirit (the literal string differs since this dialect's
own `typeName()` values are short aliases, not FLTK's `Fl_`-prefixed
names -- `pixmapFor()` resolves both forms, see its own doc comment).
The Code group's `NodeBrowser.build()` handles the whole `projectRoots_`
list, so a node with no enclosing window can be created from here too.
}
  {
    in_source
    in_header
  }
decl {void delegate(Widget) binButtonCb;}
  {
    public
    global
  }
decl {void delegate(Widget) codeButtonCb;}
  {
